          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"von-arnim-vera-7cbae446cf41cde7\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Wemyss directly avows love as the content of his present thought. The reflective framing strongly supports T; the utterance reassures Lucy but does not itself undertake a commitment, and nothing indicates reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A directly spoken avowal containing “I love you” as an embedded complement of “I'm only thinking that.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Wemyss offers the avowal after reflecting that Lucy can hurt him because his love is great. His touch and solemn gaze make it affectionate and reassuring, and Lucy receives it as confirmation that he is a sound, simple lover. The surrounding narration also places the declaration amid his possessiveness, sensitivity to perceived slights, and controlling conduct, creating narrative irony without changing the utterance's core truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I'm only thinking that I love you,” he said.\",\"supports\":\"The explicit cognitive framing presents love as a state or proposition Wemyss currently entertains and avows, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He speaks while “gazing at her solemnly” and lays his hand on hers.\",\"supports\":\"The deliberate, reflective delivery weighs against construing the words as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lucy “flushed with pleasure,” calls him “My Everard,” and concludes that “At the core he was utterly her own dear lover.”\",\"supports\":\"Lucy understands the statement as a sincere-seeming avowal and reassurance, not as a new relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before speaking, Wemyss thinks that his power to be hurt is great “because his love for her was so great” and that Lucy should be careful.\",\"supports\":\"The narration independently represents him as conceiving himself to possess a strong loving state, while also exposing possessive and self-regarding context.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T adequately captures the direct avowal of a loving state. Its affectionate delivery and narratively ironic context require no additional core-force category; P and E lack independent support.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
